<!-- https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/package-validation/diagnostic-ids -->
<!--
  Deliberate API breaks, declared rather than skipped. This is the first such file in this package.

  == externalMedia gains a channelId parameter ==

  Both entries are one edit: IAriChannelsResource.CreateExternalMediaAsync gained an optional
  `string? channelId` between `data` and its cancellation token. Optional parameters are compile-time
  sugar and ApiCompat matches a member by its full signature, so the change reads as two separate
  facts about the assembly, and both are true.

  CP0002 (member removed): the nine-parameter member is genuinely absent. A caller compiled against
  2.5.3 does not bind to the ten-parameter one without a rebuild.

  CP0006 (interface member added): IAriChannelsResource is a shipped public interface and this member
  has no default implementation, so every external implementation of it — a fake, a decorator, a proxy
  — stops compiling until it adopts the parameter. Verbara.Sdk.Pro's ARI fakes are the known
  population. That cost is named in the change's Architectural Risk rather than left for a consumer to
  discover.

  The break is bought, not stumbled into. Asterisk keys an AudioSocket stream by the UUID the creator
  supplied in `data`, and mints the ARI channel id itself, so a consumer holding only a Channel.Id has
  no way to find its stream — `channelId` is the one ARI parameter that makes those two the same value.
  The alternative that breaks nothing, minting the uuid and passing it only as `data`, was measured
  working against a real Asterisk and rejected in the change proposal, because it fixes one activity and
  leaves the capability unfixed. What a consumer has to do is written down in
  docs/guides/externalmedia-channel-id-migration.md, per ADR-0028.

  This file is committed on purpose. ADR-0055 records that letting `pack` generate it on the build
  machine turns every difference into a suppression that never reaches the repository — which is
  skipping validation while it reports green. Each entry here was read before it was kept; the
  generated file was produced into a scratch path, compared entry by entry against the breaks this
  change intends, and found to hold those two and nothing else.

  These clear when PackageValidationBaselineVersion moves past the release that ships this change.
  Do not add an entry here to make a build pass; add it because the break is intended and written down.
-->
<Suppressions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0002</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>M:Verbara.Sdk.IAriChannelsResource.CreateExternalMediaAsync(System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.Threading.CancellationToken)</Target>
    <Left>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.dll</Right>
    <IsBaselineSuppression>true</IsBaselineSuppression>
  </Suppression>
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0006</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>M:Verbara.Sdk.IAriChannelsResource.CreateExternalMediaAsync(System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.String,System.Threading.CancellationToken)</Target>
    <Left>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/net10.0/Verbara.Sdk.dll</Right>
    <IsBaselineSuppression>true</IsBaselineSuppression>
  </Suppression>
</Suppressions>